# Chapter 4, sixth part: Global, Marker.  Part of the chapter source.

.h2 Marker [format-marker]
.ix Global Marker
.ix Marker settings
.ix Marker Format
.ix Marker panels, ring of
.ix Marker formats
Global, Marker holds the settings that the markers of a document take when
the marker has none of its own: how a marker is shown on the screen, the
date and the time format, the numbering of counters, the look of notes, the
keycaps and how cross-references print.  It is a ring of nineteen panels,
with the Marker Format panel first.  This section describes each panel with
every field.  The markers themselves, with the panel that each has, are in
{n:markers}.

.h3 How to reach the command [format-marker-reach]
Press {k:F12}, choose Global and then Marker (the fourth entry; the hint
line reads "Marker settings"), as described in {x:format-global-menu}.
The first panel is Marker Format.
.pic pictures/format-marker.png 5 Marker Format.

.h3 The ring of panels [format-marker-ring]
.ix Marker ring
.ix PgUp, PgDn in Marker
.ix Counter Format panels
.ix Note Format panels
.ix Keycap Format panel
.ix Marker Display Modes panels
Marker Format is the first panel and the others are reached with {k:PgUp}
(next) and {k:PgDn} (previous); the ring wraps at both ends, {k:Ins} accepts
the settings of all nineteen panels and {k:Esc} none of them.  Going on with
{k:PgUp} from Marker Format the order is this, and {k:PgDn} goes backward.
.table 5 31 21
Step | Panel | Section
1 | Marker Format | {x:format-marker-format}
2 | Marker Display Modes (1 of 3) | {x:format-marker-modes}
3 | Marker Display Modes (2 of 3) | {x:format-marker-modes}
4 | Marker Display Modes (3 of 3) | {x:format-marker-modes}
5 | Keycap Format | {x:format-marker-keycap}
6 | Endnote Format | {x:format-marker-notes}
7 | Levelnote Format | {x:format-marker-notes}
8 | Footnote Format | {x:format-marker-notes}
9 to 19 | Counter Format (11 of 11) to (1 of 11) | {x:format-marker-counters}
.end
After Counter Format (1 of 11) {k:PgUp} returns to Marker Format.  The
information fields of the Marker Format panel say where the others are: from
Marker Format, {k:PgDn} shows Counter Format (1 of 11) first and {k:PgUp}
shows Marker Display Modes (1 of 3) first.

.h3 Marker Format [format-marker-format]
.ix Display Hidden Markers
.ix Primary Note Type
.ix Date Format
.ix Time Format
The first panel has the rows below; the ones that are information (a plain
text without dots or brackets) are skipped by the cursor.
.table 21 16 20
Field | Values | Default
Display Hidden Markers? | No, Yes | No
Marker Display Modes | information | Custom
Note Format | information |
Primary Note Type | Footnotes, Levelnotes, Endnotes | Footnotes
Cross-reference Format | Tag, Headline, Both | Tag
Counter Format | information |
Keycap Format | information |
Date Format | text | Month D, Year
Time Format | text | H:MM am/pm
.end
{b:Display Hidden Markers?}  With Yes the markers whose display mode is
Hidden are drawn on the screen in the Short form, so that they can be found
and edited; with No they are not drawn.  It is a setting of the screen: a
Hidden marker is never printed by the rules of {x:format-marker-modes}.

{b:Primary Note Type.}  The kind of note that a new Note marker is until its
own Type field says otherwise: Footnotes, Levelnotes or Endnotes.  The
Type list of the Note panel has an entry in brackets as its
first choice, and a note that has that entry takes the primary type ({n:markers},
Note).

{b:Cross-reference Format.}  Tag, Headline or Both.  The choice is kept in the
document.  A Cross-reference marker whose Style is [Tag] prints what this field
says; a marker with another Style prints what its own Style says
({n:marker-cross-reference}).

{b:Marker Display Modes, Note Format, Counter Format, Keycap Format.}  Four
information rows that tell which panel to go to with {k:PgUp} or {k:PgDn}.
Marker Display Modes reads "Custom (PgUp shows the modes)", Note Format "PgDn
after the counters", Counter Format "PgDn shows the counters" and Keycap Format
"PgDn after the Note formats".

{b:Date Format and Time Format.}  The text that a Date, Time, Filedate or
Filetime marker uses when its own Format field is empty
({n:format-marker-datetime}).

.h3 Date and time formats [format-marker-datetime]
.ix Date format words
.ix Time format words
.ix Month, Mon, Year, YYYY
.ix am/pm, time format
.ix Format of a date
.ix Format of a time
A date format and a time format are made of words, which are replaced by a part
of the date or the time, and of any other characters, which are kept.  The
words are not case sensitive: Month, month and MONTH are the same
word.  The words of a date, with the date 3 March 2027 as the example:
.table 9 38 10
Word | Meaning | Example
Month | Name of the month | March
Mon | The first three letters | Mar
Year | Four digits of the year | 2027
YYYY | Four digits of the year | 2027
YY | Last two digits | 27
MM | Number of the month, two digits | 03
M | Number of the month | 3
DD | Day of the month, two digits | 03
D | Day of the month | 3
.end
The words of a time, with the time 3:07:05 in the afternoon:
.table 9 38 10
Word | Meaning | Example
HH | Hour on a twelve hour clock, two digits | 03
H | Hour on a twelve hour clock | 3
MM | Minutes, two digits | 07
SS | Seconds, two digits | 05
am/pm | am before noon, pm after | pm
.end
Longer words are tried before shorter ones, so that Month is not read as Mon
and then th.  A format that has no
am/pm shows the hour from 1 to 12.  The default date format Month D, Year
gives March 3, 2027 and the default time format H:MM am/pm gives 3:07 pm.

.caution A letter that makes a word is replaced wherever it stands, also inside
  a word of your own.  The format "Today is Month D" prints "To3ay is March
  3": the d of Today is read as the day of the month.  Keep the fixed text of
  a format to characters that are not letters of a word (a slash, a colon, a
  hyphen, a comma), or leave the letters m, d, y, h and s out of it.

.h3 Marker Display Modes [format-marker-modes]
.ix Display modes
.ix Marker display modes
.ix Global display mode
.ix Diamond, display mode
.ix Name, display mode
.ix Short, display mode
.ix Long, display mode
.ix Hidden, display mode
.ix Inline, display mode
.pic pictures/format-marker-modes.png 5 Marker Display Modes (1 of 3).
Every kind of marker has a display mode that says how the markers of that kind
are drawn on the screen.  The 35 kinds are shown on three panels titled Marker
Display Modes (1 of 3), (2 of 3) and (3 of 3).  Each row is a choice field
with the name of the marker at its left.  Panel 1 has one more row at the
top, Global display mode.  The modes are these.
.table 10 50
Mode | What is drawn
Diamond | A diamond, the same for every marker of the kind.
Name | A short name in square brackets, such as [CB] or [PAGE].
Short | A short form in square brackets, such as [C1] or [Key: ENTER].
Long | The long form, such as [COUNTER1, list of books] or [COLUMN BREAK].
Hidden | Nothing (but see Display Hidden Markers).
Inline | The thing itself: the picture of an equation, a picture, a keycap or the
 typeset text of a LaTeX marker.
.end
Inline is offered only for the four kinds that are drawn as images, Equation,
Keycap, LaTeX and Picture; it is their default.  In the X11 version it shows
the image (an equation needs {c:latex} and {c:dvipng}; a keycap, a picture and
a LaTeX marker need {c:pdflatex} and {c:pdftoppm}).  Where the image cannot be
made (a program is missing, LaTeX finds a mistake, a file does not read, or the
job takes more than five seconds) the marker is shown in its Short form, never
as an empty box ({n:marker-latex-picture}).  The terminal version draws no
images: Inline shows the Short form there, and a keycap shows its legend in
reverse video.  The other modes for these four show the marker's label and
never the image.  A Counter and a Cross-reference show their text in the Long
mode, which is their default, and a Note shows its label in every mode but
Diamond.  A Note, Comment, Figure, Font, Index and Table marker is drawn in the
Short mode until the document says otherwise.

{b:Global display mode.}  The choices are n/a (the default), Diamond, Name,
Short, Long and Hidden.  When {k:Ins} is pressed with any choice but n/a the
rows of all kinds are set to that mode, whatever they show; n/a leaves them as
they are.  The field is always n/a when the panel is opened.

{b:Table of the markers.}  The table has the kind, its default mode, the
label that the Name mode shows, and the panel (1 to 3) on which the kind is.
.table 18 10 14 6
Marker | Default | Name label | Panel
Author | Long | AUTHOR | 1
Column Break | Short | CB | 1
Comment | Short | COM | 1
Counter | Long | C | 1
Cross-reference | Long | XREF | 1
Date | Long | DATE | 1
Description | Long | DESC | 1
Else | Name | ELSE | 1
Endif | Name | ENDIF | 1
Equation | Inline | EQN | 1
Figure | Short | FIG | 1
Filedate | Long | FDATE | 1
Filename | Long | FNAME | 2
Filetime | Long | FTIME | 2
Font | Short | FONT | 2
If | Long | IF | 2
Include | Long | INCLUDE | 2
Index | Short | INDEX | 2
Keep | Long | KEEP | 2
Keycap | Inline | KEY | 2
LaTeX | Inline | LaTeX | 2
Line | Short | LN | 2
Merge | Long | MERGE | 2
Note | Short | NOTE | 2
Orientation | Short | ORIENTATION | 3
Page Break | Short | PB | 3
Page Number | Name | PAGE | 3
Pathname | Long | PATH | 3
Picture | Inline | PIC | 3
Printer | Long | PRINTER | 3
Revision | Long | REV | 3
Section | Name | SECTION | 3
Table | Short | TABLE | 3
Time | Long | TIME | 3
Whitespace | Short | WS | 3
.end
The Short and Long forms of each kind are shown in {n:markers} under the
marker.  A mode that is not the default is a change of the document, like any
other setting of this ring.

.behavior The modes are for the screen.  What a marker does on a printed
  page does not depend on its mode: a Hidden marker is not printed less than a
  Long one.  A Counter and a Cross-reference print their text, a Note prints
  its mark, a Keep marker is not printed and keeps its blocks on one page, a
  Comment is printed in a draft print only, and so on, as {n:markers} says for
  each marker.

.h3 Counter Format [format-marker-counters]
.ix Counter Format
.ix Counter tags
.ix Counter description
.ix Numbering with counters
.ix \1\ tag
.pic pictures/format-counters.png 5 Counter Format (1 of 11).
A Counter marker ({n:markers}) numbers the items of a list, a figure, an exercise;
there are 99 counters, and the Counter Format panels define them.  Eleven
panels, titled Counter Format (1 of 11) to (11 of 11), have nine counters each:
C1 to C9, C10 to C18, and so on to C91 to C99.  Each counter has two text
fields.
.table 20 14 12
Field | Values | Default
C<n> Description | text | see below
C<n> Tag | text | \1\.
.end
{b:Description.}  A comment for the reader, never printed.  C1 is described
Numeric and C2 Alphabetic, and the others have no description.  It shows in
the label of a Counter marker in the Long form, as in [COUNTER1, list of books].

{b:Tag.}  The text that a counter prints, with the number in it written as
a code: a backslash, a letter and a backslash.  The first such group in the
tag is the number, and the letter says its style; the rest of the tag is
printed as it is.
.table 9 51
Tag | The number is written as
\1\ | Arabic numerals: 1, 2, 3.
\i\ | Lower case roman numerals: i, ii, iii.
\I\ | Upper case roman numerals: I, II, III.
\a\ | Lower case letters: a, b, c, and aa, ab after z.
\A\ | Upper case letters: A, B, C, and AA, AB after Z.
.end
The default tag of C1 is \1\. and that of C2 is \A\., which give 1. 2. 3.
and A. B. C.  The tag \1\) gives 1) and the tag Step \1\: gives Step 1:.
A tag with no group has the number put at its start.  A roman numeral is made
only for the numbers 1 to 3999; a number that a style cannot show is shown in
arabic.  Letters go on after Z with AA, AB and so on, however large the number
is.  A counter counts up to the largest number that it can hold, 4294967295
({n:markers}, the Counter marker).

{b:What a counter counts.}  The counters are numbered in the order of the
text.  The first Counter marker of a counter prints its Set Value field if it
has one and 1 if not; each later marker prints one more than the one before,
or its own Set Value.  The Number Style field of a marker ({n:markers}) changes
the style of that marker and of the later ones of the same counter, whatever
the tag says.  On the screen the number is drawn in the Long mode, and a
printed page always has it.

.h3 Note formats [format-marker-notes]
.ix Note Format
.ix Footnote Format
.ix Levelnote Format
.ix Endnote Format
.ix Reference Tag
.ix Note Tag
.ix Separator Bar
.ix Renumber
.ix Note Level
.ix Note Page Title
.ix Footnotes, endnotes, levelnotes
.pic pictures/format-footnote.png 5 Footnote Format.
There are three kinds of note and a panel for each, titled Footnote Format,
Levelnote Format and Endnote Format.  A footnote is printed at the foot of the
page that has its mark, a levelnote at the end of the section of the level
that is set on the panel (the level of its section if that is higher up), and
an endnote on a page of its own at the end of the document under a title.
.table 20 16 10 11
Field | Values | Default | Panel
Reference Tag | text | see below | all three
Reference Tag Attributes | a list | see below | all three
Note Tag | text | see below | all three
Note Tag Attributes | a list | see below | all three
Separator Bar (inches) | length | 2.000 | levelnote
Renumber? | No, Yes | No | levelnote
Note Level | 1 to 9 | 1 | levelnote
Note Page Title | text | Endnotes | endnote
.end
{b:The tags.}  The Reference Tag is the mark that stands in the text where the
note is, and the Note Tag is the label before the note itself.  Both are
written with the codes of {x:format-marker-counters} (\1\, \i\, \I\, \a\,
\A\), and any other character is printed as many times as the number of the
note: once on the first, twice on the second, and so on.  The defaults are:
.table 12 15 15 15
Kind | Reference Tag | Note Tag | Attributes
Footnote | \1\ | \1\ | Superscript; Bold
Levelnote | \a\ | \a\) | Superscript; Bold
Endnote | [\1\] | \1\. | Normal; Normal
.end
The two attribute lists have the choices Normal, Bold, Italics, Bold Italics,
Superscript, Bold Superscript, Italics Superscript and Bold Italics
Superscript.  A ninth entry, Other, is shown when a document holds an
attribute that is not one of these; leaving it alone keeps it.  Bold, italics
and superscript are the only attributes of a tag that print.  The panels
refuse nothing: a tag that is emptied, or a number that is not a number (for
the separator bar or the level, a number outside 1 to 9), keeps its old value,
and no message is shown.

{b:Separator Bar, Renumber, Note Level, Note Page Title.}  The Separator Bar
is the length of the rule that separates the notes of a level from the text.
Renumber? makes the levelnotes number again from 1 at each level in text that
is exported ({n:convert}).  Note Level is the level of section at whose end
the levelnotes are printed, and Note Page Title is the title of the page of
endnotes.  A footnote is on the page of its mark, under a short rule, and the
notes are numbered through the document or the level.

.h3 Keycap Format [format-marker-keycap]
.ix Keycap Format
.ix Keycap, default settings
.pic pictures/format-keycap.png 5 Keycap Format.
A Keycap marker draws its legend in a box, like the top of a key.  The Keycap
Format panel holds the settings that a keycap takes when its own field is left
at its bracket ({n:markers}, Keycap).
.table 16 28 13
Field | Values | Default
Foreground | 0.0 to 100.0 | 0.0
Background | 0.0 to 100.0 | 100.0
Scale | 0.100 to 10.000 | 1.000
Font | a font of the list | Text
.end
Foreground and Background are the grey of the legend and of the box, from 0.0
(black) to 100.0 (white), in tenths; Scale is the size of the keycap in
relation to the text around it.  Font is a choice of the twenty fonts, shown by
the role or the number and the name of the font in the list of the Document
Manager's TeX Font Association ({n:str}), such as "Text (Latin Modern
Roman)" or "Font 4 (Times)"; Text is the font number 2.

A value outside the range, or one that is not a number, is refused with a
message ({n:format-messages}): the panel stays open and the box shows
which values are right.  The panel of a Keycap marker rejects its own values
in the same way ({n:markers-messages}).

The Keycap Format is used by the picture that the graphical version draws for
a keycap on the screen and by the page that is printed: a keycap is drawn with
the values that the marker itself holds (its own Foreground, Background, Scale
and Font), and where it holds none, with the values of the Keycap Format.  A
document that has no Keycap Format draws black on white, scale 1.000 and the
Text font.
